---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<!-- TODO: HTTP-equiv for download -->
<Layout title={"Thank You for Downloading! (Windows)"}>
	<meta http-equiv="refresh" content="0;https://github.com/BuildABrowser/BuildABrowser/releases/download/0.1.0/Browser-0.1.0-Windows.jar" slot="head"></meta>
	<div class="semicentered section">
		<h2>Thank You for Downloading!</h2>
		<p>
			Your download should begin shortly. If it does not, click <a href="https://github.com/BuildABrowser/BuildABrowser/releases/download/0.1.0/Browser-0.1.0-Windows.jar">here</a>.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div id="command" class="semicentered section">
		<p>
			You need a Java 24+ JRE (or JDK) to run BuildABrowser. <a href="https://www.azul.com/downloads/?package=jdk#zulu">Download Azul Zulu OpenJDK</a>.
			Ensure your <span class="small-code">PATH</span> and <span class="small-code">JAVA_HOME</span> environment variables are set.
		</p>
		<code>
			java -jar Browser-0.1.0-windows.jar
		</code>
		<p>
			By default, BuildABrowser uses Skija (GPU-accelerated) for drawing. You can use Java2D by adding the <span class="small-code">-gbe java2d</span> flag,
			or use Skija in software-rendering mode via the <span class="small-code">-gbe skija-nogpu</span> flag.
		</p>
	</div>
	<div class="semicentered section">
		<a href="/"><button class="nav-button ac-1">Back to Main Page</button></a>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	#command p {
		max-width: 55em;
	}
	#command code {
		white-space: nowrap;
		max-width: 100%;
		overflow: auto;
		display: block;
	}
</style>
